cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If this tool informs a paper, a thesis, a lecture or a studio brief, please cite it as software."
type: software
title: "River Flow Lab: An interactive teaching tool for river hydraulics and design"
version: 0.1.0
abstract: >-
  A teaching tool for architecture and urban design students that shows how a
  river's water level and speed respond to its flow and to what is built in
  and beside it: obstructions, bridges, flood walls, buildings and floating
  objects. It runs entirely in the browser, shipped as a single self-contained
  HTML file. The water surface is solved for one steady flow by the
  standard-step method in one dimension, following HEC-RAS's steady-flow
  method: conveyance subdivision with Manning's equation, contraction and
  expansion losses, the energy method through a bridge with Yarnell's pier
  equation alongside, a broad-crested weir over an overtopped deck, and eddy
  zones sized by expansion and contraction ratios. Every result is read as a
  change against the same river without the design. The river can be a
  template reach, a line drawn on a map or taken from OpenStreetMap, a terrain
  model, or surveyed sections, and a flow can come from a simple float survey.
  With the bed's material chosen, it shows where the flow can move the bed.
  The method notes in docs/method-notes.html document every assumption, name
  every source the code implements, and declare what the tool does not model.
  It is a teaching model, not a flood risk assessment.
